@section('content')
  <h1>Components and reuse</h1>
  <p>Propel reuses markup in three ways today: layouts with sections, <code>@@include</code> for partial views, and stacks for content that a page adds to the layout. Each is shown with examples on the <a href="/docs/layouts">layouts page</a>.</p>

  <h2>Layouts</h2>
  <p>A layout holds the parts every page shares. It marks the place for each section with <code>@@yield</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-blade">@{{-- views/layouts/app.propel --}}
&lt;main&gt;
  @@yield('content')
&lt;/main</code></pre>
  <p>A page fills the layout by naming the layout with <code>@@extends</code> and writing its sections:</p>
<pre><code class="language-blade">@@extends('layouts.app')

@@section('content')
  &lt;h1&gt;Posts&lt;/h1&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>

  <h2>Partials with <code>@@include</code></h2>
  <p><code>@@include('partials.badge')</code> renders the partial in place, with the same data as the page.</p>
<pre><code class="language-blade">@@include('partials.badge')</code></pre>

  <h2>Planned: component tags</h2>
  <p>The next feature for reuse is a component tag, such as <code>&lt;x-card&gt;</code>, that takes attributes and a slot. It is not built yet, so the example below is only a sketch of the intended syntax:</p>
<pre><code class="language-blade">&lt;x-card title="Hello"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Card body.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/x-card&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Until component tags exist, use <code>@@include</code> with data from the page's context.</p>
@endsection
